"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][8]={"university_physics_en:M8-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M8-L1 – First-Order Linear Differential Equations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Mathematical Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Differentiation, product rule,\ndefinite integrals and the exponential function. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive an integrating factor,\nimpose an initial condition and assess the long-term limit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From Physical Law to Differential Equation<\/h2>\n<p>Many time-dependent physical systems do not respond instantaneously\nto an input. The temperature of a body, capacitor charge or a simple\ndamping variable may approach equilibrium under appropriate modeling\nassumptions. The mathematical form is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&#39;(t)+p(t)y(t)=q(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe unknown\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepends on time, while the coefficients\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare prescribed. The differential equation alone generally specifies a\nfamily of solutions. An initial value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t_0)=y_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nselects a definite trajectory when the coefficients satisfy suitable\ncontinuity conditions. A physical model must distinguish the evolving\nstate variable from the external drive and must describe which effects\nhave been deliberately neglected.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Integrating Factor: Derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Choose the integrating factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu(t)=\\exp[\\int_{t_0}^{t}p(s)\\,\\mathrm ds]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu&#39;=p\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe product rule immediately yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mu y)&#39;=\\mu y&#39;+\\mu&#39; y=\\mu q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIntegrating from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the initial-value solution:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=\\mu(t)^{-1}\\left[y_0+\\int_{t_0}^{t}\\mu(s)q(s)\\,\\mathrm ds\\right].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For constant coefficients\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=a&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis reduces to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=b/a+(y_0-b/a)e^{-a(t-t_0)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe steady value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_\\infty=b/a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the relaxation time is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=1/a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nExponential relaxation follows from this particular model and its\ninitial condition; it is not a universal feature of every relaxation\nprocess.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Exponential Approach<\/h2>\n<p>Consider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&#39;+2y=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(0)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere time is measured in seconds. The factor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=e^{2t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(e^{2t}y)&#39;=4e^{2t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\none obtains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=2+Ce^{-2t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe initial value gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand therefore<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=2-e^{-2t}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.8647<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\approx1.8647<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\to\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproaches the value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom below. Direct substitution checks the result:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&#39;=2e^{-2t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&#39;+2y=2e^{-2t}+4-2e^{-2t}=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows this rising curve and its horizontal equilibrium line.\nDimensional consistency additionally requires the coefficient\nmultiplying\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand represented by the number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto have units\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile the drive must have units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y/\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_194_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Time trace y equals two minus exponential minus two t, approaching limit two\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Time trace y equals two minus exponential\nminus two t, approaching limit two<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A solution approaches its steady-state limit from below.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Solve\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&#39;+3y=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the relaxation time for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&#39;+4y=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured in seconds.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Verify by differentiation whether\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=2-e^{-2t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&#39;+2y=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=2(1-e^{-3t})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=1/4\\,\\mathrm s=0.25\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&#39;=2e^{-2t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nadding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2y=4-2e^{-2t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&#39;+2y=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Existence, Units and Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The integrating-factor method solves linear first-order equations\nwith prescribed coefficients, not arbitrary nonlinear dynamics. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis discontinuous, existence requires additional care. A negative\nconstant coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduces growth rather than relaxation, and time-dependent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngenerally prevents assigning a single constant relaxation time.\nPhysically, one must also verify that the assumed linear response\nremains appropriate over the entire state interval. A mathematical\nsolution should not be extrapolated over temperatures, voltages or\nconcentrations for which the constitutive relation used to derive the\nequation has ceased to apply.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The product rule gives the integrating factor; the initial condition\nfixes the constant. Several coupled state variables lead next to matrix\nmethods.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Linear Differential-Equation\nSystems and Eigenmodes<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-03-differential-equations-spring-2010/pages/lecture-notes/\">18.03 Differential Equations – Lecture Notes (2010)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LEC 1, 4, 20–28, 35–38; Subject relevance to lesson: M8-L1 – First-Order Linear Differential Equations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M8-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M8-L2 – Linear Differential-Equation Systems and Eigenmodes<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Mathematical Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Linear algebra, eigenvalues,\nderivatives and first-order equations. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain the matrix exponential\nas a solution and evaluate stable eigenmodes separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why Physical Systems Are Coupled<\/h2>\n<p>A multiple-degree-of-freedom spring system, an electrical network or\na kinetic reaction model has several coupled state variables. Collect\nthem in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol{x}(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand use a linear time-invariant model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒃<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot{\\boldsymbol{x}}=A\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{b}(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMatrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes coupling and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒃<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol{b}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes external drive. Unlike a single scalar equation, changing one\ncomponent can alter the time derivative of another. An initial-value\nvector supplies all initial state information. If all state components\nuse comparable units, the entries of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave inverse-time units; for mixed physical variables each row must be\nchecked separately for dimensional consistency.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Matrix Exponential and Eigenvectors<\/h2>\n<p>For constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe convergent series\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>!<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{At}=I+At+(At)^2/2!+\\cdots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndefines the matrix exponential. Differentiation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm d(e^{At})/\\mathrm dt=Ae^{At}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe unforced initial-value solution is therefore<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol{x}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\boldsymbol{x}(t_0).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas enough independent eigenvectors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol{v}_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfying\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\boldsymbol{v}_j=\\lambda_j\\boldsymbol{v}_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe initial state can be decomposed into modes:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol{x}(t)=\\sum_j c_j e^{\\lambda_j(t-t_0)}\\boldsymbol{v}_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNegative real parts of all eigenvalues imply asymptotic decay of the\nhomogeneous system; purely imaginary eigenvalues alone do not\ndemonstrate damping.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Two Decay Modes<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-3\\end{pmatrix}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol{x}(0)=(2,1)^\\mathsf{T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe modes are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1(t)=2e^{-t},\\qquad x_2(t)=e^{-3t}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At one second,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.7358<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1\\approx0.7358<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.04979<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2\\approx0.04979<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe second component decays substantially faster even though both\napproach zero. The figure compares the two time scales. The eigenvalues\nare\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_1=-1\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_2=-3\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA measured physical signal that combines both modes need not resemble a\nsingle exponential decay. Projection onto eigenvectors separates the\ncomponents when the matrix can be diagonalized.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_157_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two decaying eigenmodes of a diagonal system matrix\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two decaying eigenmodes of a diagonal\nsystem matrix<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The faster mode decays much earlier.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Solve\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=-2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(0)=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What mode time constant corresponds to eigenvalue\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can every real matrix be diagonalized using real eigenvectors?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=3e^{-2t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=1/3\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Defective matrices may lack sufficient independent eigenvectors,\nand complex eigenvalues can also occur.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of Modal Analysis<\/h2>\n<p>Not every matrix is diagonalizable. A Jordan block can generate terms\nsuch as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t e^{\\lambda t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand nonnormal matrices can exhibit transient growth even when their\neigenvalues are stable. Time-dependent coefficients are not generally\nsolved by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{\\int A(t)\\,\\mathrm dt}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause matrices at distinct times need not commute. An external drive\nrequires a variation-of-constants or Duhamel formula rather than\nhomogeneous eigenmodes alone. Mechanical state vectors may mix positions\nand velocities; statements about the stability of equations obtained by\nlinearization initially apply only near the equilibrium around which the\napproximation was constructed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The matrix exponential generalizes scalar exponential dynamics to\ncoupled linear systems. Periodic signals additionally invite a spectral\ndescription.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Fourier Series and the Continuous\nFourier Transform<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-03-differential-equations-spring-2010/pages/lecture-notes/\">18.03 Differential Equations – Lecture Notes (2010)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LEC 1, 4, 20–28, 35–38; Subject relevance to lesson: M8-L2 – Linear Differential-Equation Systems and Eigenmodes · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M8-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M8-L3 – Fourier Series and the Continuous Fourier Transform<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Mathematical Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Trigonometry, integration and complex\nexponentials. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Find Fourier coefficients from\northogonality and distinguish spectra from reconstruction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Periodic Signals as Mode Sums<\/h2>\n<p>A periodic oscillation may look complicated while being assembled\nfrom simple harmonic components. For suitably regular periodic\nfunctions, a Fourier series represents the signal as a superposition of\nharmonics with frequencies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\omega_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor period\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nchoose the basis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1,\\cos(nx),\\sin(nx)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFourier coefficients measure contributions of these modes; they do not\nrepresent additional physical forces. In experimental physics spectra\nreveal harmonics, resonances and periodic interference. However, a\nfrequency spectrum must always be read together with its normalization,\nrecording duration and Fourier convention; otherwise even amplitude\nvalues may not be directly comparable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Coefficients from Orthogonality<\/h2>\n<p>Orthogonality on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[-\\pi,\\pi]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>π<\/mi><\/msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\sin(nx)\\sin(mx)\\,\\mathrm dx=\\pi\\delta_{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor positive integers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n,m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn analogous statement holds for cosine modes, while mixed integrals\nvanish. Consequently,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∼<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)\\sim\\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left[a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\right],<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>π<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n=\\pi^{-1}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\mathrm dx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>π<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_n=\\pi^{-1}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\mathrm dx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt a jump discontinuity, under the usual Dirichlet conditions the series\nconverges to the average of the two one-sided limits. This is a property\nof convergence, not a failure of integration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: The Sawtooth Function<\/h2>\n<p>Take the odd function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\pi&lt;x&lt;\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand extend it periodically. Symmetry makes all cosine coefficients\nvanish. Integration by parts yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>π<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_n=\\pi^{-1}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}x\\sin(nx)\\,\\mathrm dx=2(-1)^{n+1}/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∼<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)\\sim 2\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\sin(nx).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe function and series agree, whereas at the jump\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe Fourier series takes the average value zero. The diagram compares\nthe sawtooth with a finite partial sum and displays the overshoot near\nthe discontinuity. Adding more harmonics narrows that overshoot\nspatially but does not eliminate its limiting relative amplitude.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_104_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Sawtooth curve and a Fourier partial sum with overshoot near a jump\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Sawtooth curve and a Fourier partial sum\nwith overshoot near a jump<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Finite partial sum and periodic target function.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What cosine Fourier coefficients\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes the odd function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the sawtooth coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What value does the series take at the jump\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">All\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nincluding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_2=2(-1)^3/2=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The average of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnamely\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Continuous Spectrum and Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Nonperiodic signals often call for a continuous spectrum. One\nconvention is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>f<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat f(k)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)e^{-ikx}\\,\\mathrm dx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mover><mi>f<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-1}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\hat f(k)e^{ikx}\\,\\mathrm dk<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese integrals require suitable integrability or distributional\nassumptions. A recording of finite duration never yields the ideal\nspectrum of an infinitely long signal: windowing introduces spectral\nleakage. Fourier transformation is linear, so the spectrum of a sum is\nthe sum of complex spectra. Interference between contributions can\nemerge after taking absolute values. A measured power spectrum generally\nlacks the full phase information required for a unique\nreconstruction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Orthogonality produces Fourier coefficients. The next method often\nhandles time-domain initial-value problems more easily in the Laplace\ndomain.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Laplace Transforms, Transfer\nFunctions and Convolution<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-03-differential-equations-spring-2010/pages/lecture-notes/\">18.03 Differential Equations – Lecture Notes (2010)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LEC 1, 4, 20–28, 35–38; Subject relevance to lesson: M8-L3 – Fourier Series and the Continuous Fourier Transform · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/1Bmethods.html\">University of Cambridge – Mathematical Methods<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Contents: Fourier Series, Sturm–Liouville Theory, Distributions, Green’s Functions, Fourier Transforms, Method of Characteristics; Subject relevance to lesson: M8-L3 – Fourier Series and the Continuous Fourier Transform · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M8-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M8-L4 – Laplace Transforms, Transfer Functions and Convolution<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Mathematical Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Integrating factors, exponential\nfunctions, integration and complex numbers. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Solve causal linear\ninitial-value problems with Laplace transforms and interpret\nconvolution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Causal Signals and the Transform Domain<\/h2>\n<p>A dynamic experiment often starts at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\na switch is closed, a detector is pulsed or a driving input steps to a\nconstant value. For these causal signals, the unilateral Laplace\ntransform is useful. It maps a time trace\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(s)=\\int_0^{\\infty}f(t)e^{-st}\\,\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis generally complex, and the integral converges only in a specified\nregion of the complex plane. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(t)=e^{-at}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe transform is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(s)=1/(s+a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Re}(s)&gt;-a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLaplace and Fourier transforms are related, but they play different\nroles in initial-value analysis and stationary spectral\ndescriptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Derivative Rule and Convolution Theorem<\/h2>\n<p>Integration by parts gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L\\{f&#39;\\}(s)=sF(s)-f(0^+)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor suitably regular functions. A constant-coefficient linear\ndifferential equation thereby becomes an algebraic equation for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&#39;+ay=u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y(s)=U(s)/(s+a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe transfer function is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(s)=1/(s+a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the time domain, the convolution theorem states<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=(h*u)(t)=\\int_0^t h(t-\\tau)u(\\tau)\\,\\mathrm d\\tau,\\qquad h(t)=e^{-at}\\quad(t\\geq0).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Thus the output sums delayed responses to all earlier input\ncontributions. The impulse response is not an ordinary finite-duration\ninput pulse, but the mathematically defined response to an ideal delta\nexcitation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Response to a Step<\/h2>\n<p>Solve\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&#39;+2y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe transform of the constant right-hand side is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y(s)=1/[s(s+2)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPartial fractions give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y(s)=\\tfrac12(1/s-1/(s+2))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhose inverse transform is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=\\frac12(1-e^{-2t}).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.43233<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\approx0.43233<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the long-time value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe same function results by convolving impulse response\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-2t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the constant input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(t)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram contrasts the decreasing impulse response with the\nincreasing step response. They share the time constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=0.5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthough their physical units need not agree under an arbitrary input\nnormalization.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_143_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Normalized impulse response and step response of a linear system\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Normalized impulse response and step\nresponse of a linear system<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Shared time constant, different signal profiles.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L\\{e^{-3t}\\}(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Solve\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&#39;+4y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is impulse response\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuseful for arbitrary inputs of linear time-invariant systems?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(s+3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Re}(s)&gt;-3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=(1-e^{-4t})/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For a causal system initially at rest and input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(t)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe output is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=\\int_0^t h(t-\\tau)u(\\tau)\\,\\mathrm d\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Poles, Stability and Modeling Conditions<\/h2>\n<p>Transfer functions describe linear time-invariant systems with\nspecified inputs and outputs. Pole positions and multiplicities encode\nhomogeneous modes, but simplistic stability claims must account for\npossible pole-zero cancellations and internal states. The unilateral\ntransform includes initial conditions, whereas the bilateral transform\nhas a different definition. Ideal impulses and steps are naturally\ntreated with distributions. Numerical inversion can be difficult,\nespecially with noisy data; a measured frequency response by itself does\nnot automatically yield a reliable causal model. A transfer function is\na model representation, not a substitute for experimental\nvalidation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Laplace transformation converts time derivatives and convolution into\nalgebraic operations. Spatial dependence leads next to partial\ndifferential equations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Partial Differential Equations:\nHeat and Waves<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-03-differential-equations-spring-2010/pages/lecture-notes/\">18.03 Differential Equations – Lecture Notes (2010)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LEC 1, 4, 20–28, 35–38; Subject relevance to lesson: M8-L4 – Laplace Transforms, Transfer Functions and Convolution · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M8-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M8-L5 – Partial Differential Equations: Heat and Waves<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Mathematical Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Partial derivatives, Fourier modes,\ninitial and boundary data. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish heat and wave\nequations and obtain a separated modal solution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Spatially Distributed States<\/h2>\n<p>An ordinary differential equation describes finitely many evolving\nstate variables. In a rod or vibrating string, the unknown depends on\nposition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe excess temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(x,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand string displacement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(x,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfy different partial differential equations. Initial data specify\nthe state at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile boundary conditions prescribe behavior at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout these additional conditions, a formal equation may admit many\nphysically distinct solutions. For an insulated rod, the heat flux at\nthe ends vanishes; for ends maintained at fixed reference temperature,\nthe excess temperature at the ends vanishes instead. These models must\nnot be conflated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Heat Conduction and Wave Propagation<\/h2>\n<p>For constant material parameters and one-dimensional heat transport,\nthe diffusion equation is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>∂<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t}=\\kappa\\frac{\\partial^2u}{\\partial x^2},<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>with thermal diffusivity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>κ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m^2/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts time derivative is first order, and thermal contrasts smooth out.\nFor an ideal string, by contrast,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>∂<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>∂<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial^2u}{\\partial t^2}=c^2\\frac{\\partial^2u}{\\partial x^2},<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a wave speed. A second time derivative appears, allowing local\ndisturbances to propagate as waves. Both equations permit separation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(x,t)=X(x)T(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor suitable homogeneous boundary conditions, but this leads to an\nexponential time factor for heat and oscillatory time dependence for\nundamped waves.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: A Decaying Temperature Mode<\/h2>\n<p>Consider a rod of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhose ends remain at the reference temperature. For initial data\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(x,0)=U_0\\sin(\\pi x/L)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe solution is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(x,t)=U_0\\sin(\\pi x/L)\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\kappa\\pi^2t}{L^2}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=0.01\\,\\mathrm{m^2/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=10\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe maximum excess temperature falls by the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.01<\/mn><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.3727<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-0.01\\pi^2\\cdot10}\\approx0.3727<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows the same spatial sine mode at three times: its shape is\npreserved for this single eigenmode, but the amplitude decays. A general\ninitial temperature profile is normally a sum of modes, with higher\nspatial frequencies decaying faster because of the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_129_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Decaying spatial sine profile at three times in a rod\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Decaying spatial sine profile at three\ntimes in a rod<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A single eigenmode retains its spatial sine shape.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the SI unit of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>κ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the heat equation?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By what factor does the second spatial heat mode decay relative to\nits initial amplitude after time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the wave equation require two temporal initial\nconditions?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m^2/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mi>κ<\/mi><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp(-4\\kappa\\pi^2t/L^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Because it is second order in time; both initial displacement and\ninitial velocity are needed.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Boundary Conditions and Numerical Cautions<\/h2>\n<p>The classical heat equation is a continuum diffusion model. Its\nmathematical solutions can become nonzero at distant points for\narbitrarily small positive times, so it is not an exact relativistic\nsignal-propagation theory. Other transport models may be required at\nvery short times or under steep gradients. The undamped wave equation\nneglects friction and material dispersion. Dirichlet, Neumann and mixed\nboundary conditions produce different allowable eigenfunctions.\nNumerically, grid spacing and time step must also satisfy stability\nrequirements involving diffusivity or wave speed; a visually plausible\nresult is not sufficient numerical validation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Spatial eigenfunctions and time evolution separate for simple linear\nboundary problems. Sturm–Liouville theory provides their mathematical\nbasis.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Sturm–Liouville Problems and\nOrthogonal Eigenfunctions<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-303-linear-partial-differential-equations-fall-2006/pages/syllabus/\">18.303 Linear Partial Differential Equations – Syllabus (2006)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Outline I–VI; Subject relevance to lesson: M8-L5 – Partial Differential Equations: Heat and Waves · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/1Bmethods.html\">University of Cambridge – Mathematical Methods<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Contents: Fourier Series, Sturm–Liouville Theory, Distributions, Green’s Functions, Fourier Transforms, Method of Characteristics; Subject relevance to lesson: M8-L5 – Partial Differential Equations: Heat and Waves · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M8-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M8-L6 – Sturm–Liouville Problems and Orthogonal Eigenfunctions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Mathematical Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Second-order differential equations,\nintegration by parts and trigonometry. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine eigenvalues and\neigenfunctions with boundary conditions and compute orthogonality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Eigenvalues from Boundary Conditions<\/h2>\n<p>For a stretched string with both ends fixed, not every spatial\nvibration profile is possible. Boundary conditions select discrete\neigenfunctions. The same principle appears in heat conduction, acoustic\ncavities, electromagnetic resonators and quantum wavefunctions. A\ntypical separated partial differential equation yields the spatial\nequation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-X&#39;&#39;=\\lambda X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe parameter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncannot be chosen arbitrarily if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X(0)=X(L)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the solution is required to be nonzero. Eigenvalue problems connect\ndifferential equations to spectral expansions. Eigenvalues belong to an\noperator together with its domain and boundary conditions, not merely to\nthe symbolic differential equation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A Self-Adjoint Differential Operator<\/h2>\n<p>A regular Sturm–Liouville problem takes the form<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}\\!\\left(p(x)\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right)+q(x)y=\\lambda w(x)y,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>with suitable real\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand self-adjoint boundary conditions. Multiply equations for two\neigenfunctions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_m,y_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncrosswise, subtract and integrate over the interval. Integration by\nparts makes the boundary term vanish when the conditions are\nappropriate. It follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\lambda_m-\\lambda_n)\\int_0^L w(x)y_m(x)y_n(x)\\,\\mathrm dx=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDistinct eigenvalues therefore imply orthogonality with respect to the\nweighted inner product:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^L w(x)y_m(x)y_n(x)\\,\\mathrm dx=0\\quad(m\\ne n).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The weight function is an essential part of the problem and must not\nbe silently removed during normalization.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Fixed Endpoints<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=w=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(0)=y(L)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe problem is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-y&#39;&#39;=\\lambda y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNonzero solutions exist for<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>L<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>L<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_n=\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}\\right)^2,\\qquad y_n(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac2L}\\sin\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right),\\quad n=1,2,\\ldots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>They are normalized by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^L y_m y_n\\,\\mathrm dx=\\delta_{mn}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe fundamental eigenvalue is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.4674<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_1=\\pi^2/4\\,\\mathrm{m^{-2}}\\approx2.4674\\,\\mathrm{m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows the first three spatial eigenmodes, including their end\nnodes. The number of interior nodes increases with mode index. In the\nwave equation, these eigenvalues produce angular frequencies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><msqrt><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_n=c\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nin the heat equation, they control decay rates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa\\lambda_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_287_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"First three sine eigenfunctions with fixed endpoints and increasing interior nodes\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">First three sine eigenfunctions with\nfixed endpoints and increasing interior nodes<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Every eigenmode vanishes at the endpoints.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the eigenvalue\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the interior zeros of mode\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;x&lt;L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the general inner product contain the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_2=4\\pi^2\\,\\mathrm{m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=L/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2L/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Orthogonality follows from the eigenvalue equation with weight\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot from an unweighted product.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Weight Functions and Scope<\/h2>\n<p>The convenient sine basis assumes constant coefficients and simple\nendpoint conditions. For spatially varying\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe eigenfunctions are generally not pure sine waves. Completeness of an\neigenbasis is a mathematical theorem with assumptions; it is not a\nconsequence of orthogonality alone. Singular endpoints or\nnon-self-adjoint conditions can produce continuous spectra or complex\neigenvalues. Physical interpretation of the norm also depends on the\nunits assigned to the state field. A measured profile can be\napproximated using eigenfunctions, but noise and finite spatial\nresolution limit the number of modes that can be reliably\nreconstructed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Boundary conditions select an orthogonal set of spatial eigenmodes.\nComplex analysis provides another tool for evaluating certain integrals:\nresidues.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Complex Analysis and the Residue\nTheorem<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-303-linear-partial-differential-equations-fall-2006/pages/syllabus/\">18.303 Linear Partial Differential Equations – Syllabus (2006)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Outline I–VI; Subject relevance to lesson: M8-L6 – Sturm–Liouville Problems and Orthogonal Eigenfunctions · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/1Bmethods.html\">University of Cambridge – Mathematical Methods<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Contents: Fourier Series, Sturm–Liouville Theory, Distributions, Green’s Functions, Fourier Transforms, Method of Characteristics; Subject relevance to lesson: M8-L6 – Sturm–Liouville Problems and Orthogonal Eigenfunctions · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M8-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M8-L7 – Complex Analysis and the Residue Theorem<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Mathematical Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Complex numbers, integration,\ndifferentiation and rational functions. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Identify simple poles, calculate\nresidues and evaluate a real integral by contour closure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why Use the Complex Plane?<\/h2>\n<p>Physics often involves integrals over frequency, momentum or energy\nthat are cumbersome to evaluate directly on the real axis. Extending the\nvariable to the complex plane lets poles and analytic domains encode\nimportant information about the integral. A complex variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=x+iy<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas two real components. Holomorphic functions possess a\ndirection-independent complex derivative, permitting stronger integral\nresults than are available for arbitrary real functions of two\nvariables. Rational functions provide a concrete example: zeros of the\ndenominator are isolated singularities in the complex plane. Which of\nthese singularities lies inside an integration contour determines the\ncontour integral. Complex methods do not, however, replace a careful\ncheck of limiting operations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Analytic Functions and Residues<\/h2>\n<p>A function has a simple pole at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen locally\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(z)=a_{-1}/(z-z_0)+a_0+a_1(z-z_0)+\\cdots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCoefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the residue. At a simple pole it is often found from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Res<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Res}(f,z_0)=\\lim_{z\\to z_0}(z-z_0)f(z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe residue theorem states<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∮<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒞<\/mi><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Res<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\oint_{\\mathcal C} f(z)\\,\\mathrm dz=2\\pi i\\sum_{z_k\\,\\mathrm{in}}\\operatorname{Res}(f,z_k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>for a positively oriented closed contour with no singularity on the\ncontour and the usual analyticity assumptions. A real integral follows\nonly if any auxiliary contour segment can be controlled and its\ncontribution vanishes in the desired limit or can be included\nexplicitly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: A Lorentzian Integral<\/h2>\n<p>Evaluate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\mathrm dx/(x^2+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWrite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(z)=1/[(z-i)(z+i)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand close the real axis with an upper semicircle of radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOnly pole\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis enclosed, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Res<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Res}(f,i)=1/(2i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe arc length is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f(z)|\\leq1/(R^2-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non that arc, so its integral decreases at least as fast as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=2\\pi i\\cdot\\frac{1}{2i}=\\pi.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The figure marks the positive contour orientation and both poles;\nonly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis inside the upper contour. Substituting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=a u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields the general result\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>a<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}a\\,\\mathrm dx/(x^2+a^2)=\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_047_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Semicircular contour enclosing the pole plus i with pole minus i outside\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Semicircular contour enclosing the pole\nplus i with pole minus i outside<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Only the upper-half-plane pole is enclosed.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the poles of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(z^2+4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute the residue of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(z^2+4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=2i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Evaluate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}2\\,\\mathrm dx/(x^2+4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=2i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=-2i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(4i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nby substitution or residues.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Choosing a Contour and Avoiding Errors<\/h2>\n<p>The contour must be chosen for the integrand. In Fourier integrals\ncontaining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{ikz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe sign of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand exponential decay govern whether an upper or lower half-plane\ncontour is appropriate. A pole on the real path requires a separate\nintegration prescription, such as a defined principal value or an\nexplicit pole displacement. Branch points require cuts rather than\nsimple residue sums. Even for rational functions one must verify that\nthe semicircular arc contributes nothing; the present estimate cannot be\ncopied to integrands that decay more slowly. Mathematical conventions\ndetermine the orientation and can change apparent signs or factors of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Residues exploit analytic structure to compute integrals. A related\nidea for differential operators is to construct their response to a\npoint source as a Green function.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Green Functions and Delta\nDistributions<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-04-complex-variables-with-applications-spring-2018/pages/lecture-notes/\">18.04 Complex Variables with Applications – Lecture Notes (2018)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topics 7–9, LEC 17–25; Subject relevance to lesson: M8-L7 – Complex Analysis and the Residue Theorem · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M8-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M8-L8 – Green Functions and Delta Distributions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Mathematical Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Boundary-value problems, integration,\nlinear operators and piecewise functions. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive a one-dimensional\nDirichlet Green function and integrate a source distribution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Response to a Point Source<\/h2>\n<p>A linear boundary-value problem can involve a source distributed in\nspace: electrostatic charge, mechanical loading or heat generation\nwithin a rod. Because the governing operator is linear, first determine\nits response to an idealized source localized at a single point. This\npoint response is called a Green function. The ideal source is\nrepresented by the Dirac delta distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta(x-\\xi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich selects a function value at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ξ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\xi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunder integration. It is not an ordinary infinitely tall function that\ncan be evaluated pointwise everywhere. Its precise use belongs to\nintegrals or distribution equations. With homogeneous boundary\nconditions, an arbitrary distributed source can be reconstructed as a\nsuperposition of these point responses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Green Function from Boundary and Jump Conditions<\/h2>\n<p>Consider operator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L=-\\mathrm d^2/\\mathrm dx^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;x&lt;L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith homogeneous Dirichlet boundaries. We seek\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(x,\\xi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfying\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\partial_x^2G(x,\\xi)=\\delta(x-\\xi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(0,\\xi)=G(L,\\xi)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAway from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\xi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis linear in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nImpose continuity at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\xi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand integrate the differential equation across a small interval around\nthat point. This yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>G<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G&#39;_+(\\xi)-G&#39;_-(\\xi)=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCombined with the endpoint values, it gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>L<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(x,\\xi)=\\frac{\\min(x,\\xi)\\,[L-\\max(x,\\xi)]}{L}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>As a function of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthis is piecewise linear with a kink at the source location. That kink\nrepresents the delta source in the distributional sense.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: A Uniform Source<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-u&#39;&#39;(x)=f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(0)=u(L)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Green function constructs<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>ξ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(x)=\\int_0^L G(x,\\xi)f(\\xi)\\,\\mathrm d\\xi.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Choose a constant source\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin consistently scaled units. Direct integration over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\xi&lt;x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&lt;\\xi&lt;L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(x)=\\frac{x(L-x)}2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Differentiating twice verifies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-u&#39;&#39;=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat both ends. Its maximum occurs at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=L/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand has value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{\\max}=L^2/8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure depicts the triangular Green function for a fixed source\npoint\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ξ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\xi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand zero endpoint values. This point response must be distinguished from\nthe parabolic solution generated by a uniform source.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_119_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Triangular Green function for the negative second-derivative operator\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Triangular Green function for the\nnegative second-derivative operator<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Continuous solution with a jump in the first derivative.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(L/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-u&#39;&#39;=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith Dirichlet boundaries.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\xi=L/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfind the maximum of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(x,\\xi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(x,\\xi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot twice classically differentiable at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\xi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(L/2)=L^2/8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(\\xi,\\xi)=\\xi(L-\\xi)/L=L/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The first derivative has a jump of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe second derivative is defined distributionally as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\delta(x-\\xi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Symmetry, Existence and Applications<\/h2>\n<p>A Green function depends on the differential operator, its domain and\nevery boundary condition. Different boundaries produce different Green\nfunctions; if the operator has a zero mode, uniqueness can fail or a\ncompatibility condition may be required. The symmetry\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(x,\\xi)=G(\\xi,x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshown here follows from this self-adjoint operator with Dirichlet\nboundaries. Time-dependent problems require retarded, advanced or other\nGreen functions according to the physical question. A retarded response\nrespects causal structure rather than merely the form of a spatial\noperator. In discrete numerical models, inverse matrices or suitable\nsolution operators play an analogous role, but the continuous delta\ndistribution must not be inserted as an ordinary single-valued\nnumber.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Green functions superpose point sources to solve linear\nboundary-value problems. The methods of M8 will recur in field theories\nand quantum mechanics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-303-linear-partial-differential-equations-fall-2006/pages/syllabus/\">18.303 Linear Partial Differential Equations – Syllabus (2006)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Outline I–VI; Subject relevance to lesson: M8-L8 – Green Functions and Delta Distributions · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/1Bmethods.html\">University of Cambridge – Mathematical Methods<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Contents: Fourier Series, Sturm–Liouville Theory, Distributions, Green’s Functions, Fourier Transforms, Method of Characteristics; Subject relevance to lesson: M8-L8 – Green Functions and Delta Distributions · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
